  <page>

    <exercise>
        <title>Conceptual Questions</title>
        <p>
            <ol>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        The time dilation effect is described as <q>moving clocks run slow</q>. 
                        Does this result have anything to do with the time it takes light to travel from the 
                        clock to your eye?
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        A clock on a moving train reads <m>T</m>. As seen from the ground, does the clock read 
                        <m>\gamma T</m> or <m>T/\gamma</m>?
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </p>
        
    </exercise>
    <exercise>
      <title>Spacetime Physics Problems</title>
        <p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        1-3
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        1-7 (feel free to use the invariant interval if you'd like or think about how to do this 
                        using time dilation/length contraction)
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        1-9 Edited a little bit: given the travel distance <m>L</m>, first obtain expressions for 
                        the rocket trip time <m>t_R</m> given the Earth trip time <m>t_E</m> and vice-versa. 
                        Also obtain the speed of the rocket in both cases.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        3-7 No need for calculations if you can clearly explain which reasoning is correct
                        and which is not.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        3-15
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        3-16 Note: It is ok to make estimates for this problem, don't try to be exact. 
                        The map in the book gives distances in milliarcseconds, which is an angle. The conversion factor to light 
                        years is the distance <m>L</m> between the quasar and Earth given in the text 
                        (you can think about the <q>distance</q> in mas as the angle between the directions where different points 
                        in the map are located, and then <m>L\theta</m> is a distance because <m>\theta</m> is very small)
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <title>Rocket Trave</title>
        <p>
            At noon a rocketship passes the earth with a velocity 0.8c. Observers on the ship and on Earth agree
            that it is noon. Let this be our t=0. It will make your life easier to measure distances in units of 
            minutes. For this problem, take the <q>Earth frame</q> to be at rest. This means you might say that
            <q>rocket clocks are slow</q> or <q>rocket distances are shorter</q> or something similar. 
        </p>
        <p>
            <ol>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        At 12:30 P.M. as read by a rocketship clock, the ship passes an interplanetary navigational
                        station that is fixed relative to the Earth and whose clocks read Earth time.
                        What time is it at the station?
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        How far from Earth (in Earth coordinates) is the station?
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        At 12:30 P.M. rocketship time the ship reports by radio back to Earth. When 
                        (by Earth time) does the Earth receive the signal?
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        The station on Earth replies immediately. When (by rocket time) is the reply received?
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </p>
    </exercise>
  </page>

  <page>
    <exercise>
        <title>Doing it again.</title>
        <p>
            Do problem 3 again, but this time take the rocket frame to be the one at rest.
        </p>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <title>Seeing vs observing and measuring</title>
        <p>
            As discussed in the text, you measure the length of a rod by finding out where its two ends are at a 
            fixed time. This requires reading some sort of report from our lattice of rods and clocks, 
            and it is not what you see with your eyes. Let's try to figure out what a Lorentz-contracted rod might 
            look like. Suppose a meter stick pointing in the x direction moves along the x axis with speed 0.8c 
            (so its length is not 1m in our frame), with its midpoint passing through the origin at t = 0.  
        </p>
        <p>
            An observer is situated very far along the x axis and a little bit below it 
            (so the stick doesn’t hit them but we won’t worry about vertical displacements). 

            <ol>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Where (in the observer’s frame) are the end points of the meter stick at t = 0?
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        When does the observer see the midpoint pass through the origin ?
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Where were the endpoints of the rod located when they emitted the light
                        that reached the observer at the same time as you found in the previous part?
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                     So if you were to take a picture of the stick with your eyes, 
                     what would be the size of the stick?
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </p>
    </exercise>
    
    <exercise>
        <title>The Doppler Effect</title>
        <p>
        A radar transmitter T is fixed to a spaceship S' which is moving to the right with speed <m>v</m> 
        relative to observers on Earth. A timer on the spaceship has a period <m>\tau_s</m> and it causes T 
        to emit radar pulses, which travel at the speed of light. The pulses are received by R on Earth 
        (See the figure below).
        </p>

        <image source="images/hwimg/hw10/doppler_sketch.svg" width="80%">
            <shortdescription>A rocket moving to the right sends radio pulses to a stationary receiver on Earth</shortdescription>
            <description>
                <p>
                    An antenna, labeled R, is fixed to the ground. A rocket labeled S' moves with speed <m>v</m> to the right.
                    The rocket has a transmitter T emitting radio pulses towards the antenna, which is to its left.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Two points on the ground, A and B, are also marked. B is to the right of A, and they are
                    separated by a distance <m>v\tau_E</m> 
                </p>
            </description>
        </image>

        <p>
            <ol>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        What would be the period <m>\tau_E</m> of the timer as seen by observers A and B on Earth, 
                        spaced a distance <m>v\tau_E</m> apart? To be clear, this means that if A sees a pulse fire when 
                        T passes A, then B would also see a pulse fire when T passes B.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Show that the receiver R would observe the time interval between pulses arriving from T not
                        as <m>τ_S</m> or <m>τ_E</m> but rather <m>\tau^{'}=τ_{S} \sqrt{\dfrac{c+v}{c-v}} </m>
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Explain why the observer at R measures a different period for the transmitter than 
                        observers A and B who are in the same reference frame. (Hint: Compare the events measured 
                        by R to the events measured by A and B.)
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </p>
    </exercise>
  </page>
</worksheet>